            {
              "text": "b2",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": ", "
            },
            {
              "text": "b6",
              "marks": ["code"]
            }
          ]
        ],
        [
          [
            {
              "text": "Locrian"
            }
          ],
          [
            {
              "text": "b2",
              "marks": [
                "code",
                {
                  "type": "color",
                  "tone": "rose"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every avoid note over each mode's own tonic triad, all seven in one place. Two things are safe to read straight off it: Lydian and Dorian are the only two of the seven with none at all, and Phrygian is the only one with two."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The Locrian row is the surprising one. Home is "
        },
        {
          "text": "A C Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". A half step above each of those three tones lands on "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "C#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Only "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b2",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "rose"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " — is actually in the scale. "
        },
        {
          "text": "C#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " never was, and "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is precisely the note Locrian gave up to get here. One avoid note, not two, not none."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the interesting near-miss. It's an avoid note in Aeolian and in Phrygian, both of which sit it a half step above a perfect fifth. Locrian's fifth is already flattened, so its own "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ") sits a "
        },
        {
          "text": "whole",
          "marks": ["bold"]
        },
        {
          "text": " step above the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "), not a half step. Same degree name, same distance from the root, and it isn't an avoid note here at all — "
        },
        {
          "text": "Where the Half Steps Fall",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-half-steps"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already said the same thing from the other side of it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That's the general point the Locrian row proves: an avoid note depends on the exact chord underneath, not on the scale carrying a note called "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " or "
        },
        {
          "text": "b2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " in the abstract. Change the shape of home and the clashes move, even though the scale is doing exactly what it always did."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every count so far has been taken over each mode's own tonic triad, because that's the chord a vamp holds. Move to the second chord instead and the answers change completely."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Take Aeolian over "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", the second chord of its own vamp. Over "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " the "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " was the one note to move through, never to land on. In "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "F A C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ") is the root of the chord under your hand. The note you had to keep moving through a moment ago is a note you can land on and hold. Nothing else in A natural minor clashes with "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " either."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Dorian runs the mirror case. Over "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " there was nothing that fought at all — that's the whole point of Dorian. But over the "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "D F# A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ") that makes "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a Dorian vamp, the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ") sits a half step above the "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". A mode with nothing that fights over home acquires something that fights the moment the second chord arrives. The major family does the same thing: Lydian's own "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ") sits a half step above the "
        },
        {
          "text": "D#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " in "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", its own vamp's second chord."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
