    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Start the drone and tap "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " against the held "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", then "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", then "
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". The drone only holds the root, not the whole chord, so tapping chips in a row isn't the clash itself. For that, hold a real "
        },
        {
          "text": "Adim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " from "
        },
        {
          "text": "Chord Shapes",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/chord-shapes"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " and let a "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ring over it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "tip",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "An avoid note isn't a fact about the scale sitting on the shelf. Ask it of the chord under your hand — and ask it again the moment the chord changes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "None of this is a different mode showing up per chord — it's the same seven notes, and which of them clash depends on what's under your hand at that instant. What to call the scale over each individual chord is a different question, and it has an answer of its own: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Two Questions That Sound Alike",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-a-different-question"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " is where the two get separated out."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
